#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cfff4db-e33e-5a3c-a1d4-99e910f2c2fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10897" :question/problem "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a3e0d847-4dd1-5a47-948c-06959065b73f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10898" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "29ca6f83-ceb9-54fa-b8c4-6c0fbbd4b742" :question/math-academy-id "e-10911" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "980ac1d7-4bcc-5375-9f73-b59394ad0f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291808} {:answer/id #uuid "8342de26-d15d-522c-9b1b-ccebcbc8ae0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291809} {:answer/id #uuid "734466e3-01c0-57c5-8305-d6ca96e26df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291810} {:answer/id #uuid "f505a544-95fe-530e-b222-dc3ff1243d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291811} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0c056e-67d3-5f35-ad10-3da44ccdddc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f505a544-95fe-530e-b222-dc3ff1243d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291811} :answer-field/id #uuid "901efb8a-2f56-5000-8e0b-e116bc3fe2c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbb96456-68ae-5b30-9f53-6b93317d471e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115256" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-x)^{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7693e9fc-715d-5d4d-8002-ce63e512ffd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291815} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc11f47-51f1-5a8c-851f-3d243ecac4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291816} {:answer/id #uuid "8e068557-ac8e-588f-a9b7-df6e0c9cc732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291817} {:answer/id #uuid "8c583a2e-1c81-531d-8430-3b23943e4231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291818} {:answer/id #uuid "f2baff23-fbf6-5a97-ad1e-e555e37302b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7693e9fc-715d-5d4d-8002-ce63e512ffd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291815} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3aeaeec-0dd5-5acd-a556-85aa12c46f76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01023191-e11f-5c48-bf20-f38a6d28c5ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-115259" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-x)^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2f0bd08-7e18-5b27-a0db-6ece8beb467e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291822} {:answer/id #uuid "fac589ec-0320-5beb-b3f0-d86ff9930e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291823} {:answer/id #uuid "9c337d29-a21a-5b03-b99e-61c285bd81c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291824} {:answer/id #uuid "4121ca88-9649-5e27-92c5-fe01186c91d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291825} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fe3ddf-aa32-55cd-b995-26d5b599206e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fac589ec-0320-5beb-b3f0-d86ff9930e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291823} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ba0e6c-800d-5c0d-bd49-ee01ed2b4ab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18225fca-59e9-551a-ae68-be859317b188" :question/math-academy-id "q-115276" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ac3bdec-788f-5a7e-ac8a-125050356bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291829} {:answer/id #uuid "4f23b530-decd-5d97-b0fa-de91d18d7097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291830} {:answer/id #uuid "b6371d58-0924-581a-8c1a-a7affe56b7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{A}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291831} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba48582-b318-544d-a31e-40eb3edd4a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291832} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e33888-b713-5a02-b5e5-6367d58fdd34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba48582-b318-544d-a31e-40eb3edd4a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291832} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c2d0fba-0bbc-5b52-9fdd-9bd106fd1b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0b99c07-f690-5bcb-a878-0a11ff2111e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-156188" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4715a737-1300-553f-b813-e1b91d681149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291836} {:answer/id #uuid "63fd1c51-8dcb-5983-bd55-8c96cca2c5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63fd1c51-8dcb-5983-bd55-8c96cca2c5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291837} :answer-field/id #uuid "f892b3e0-f4dd-5fea-aa6d-f9030612390d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c3b3633-3cca-52ce-9f8b-8508e90c46c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334352" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}sin\\,({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{1}{(-x)^{2}}sin\\,((-x)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59051da-7fc3-52c5-900e-90e4096b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a59051da-7fc3-52c5-900e-90e4096b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291840} :answer-field/id #uuid "5003cd86-c816-5b76-8f3e-ad794b0ec3b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1d28b87-b5c4-5756-ad04-9539f3fce14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334354" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{3}}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cb4c30d-be67-5a09-befb-b8ae45f6dec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cb4c30d-be67-5a09-befb-b8ae45f6dec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291843} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a2194c-858e-529f-b442-7281e9d6d9ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db66b9a5-980d-5cdb-805e-4fe14da0f316" :question/math-academy-id "q-334369" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90f2d0d8-960b-595b-a4c2-8e564e576ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291846} {:answer/id #uuid "feb7c850-20be-5332-a5de-9d8c7897b38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb7c850-20be-5332-a5de-9d8c7897b38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291847} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb2ac4f7-d05e-5e07-be02-0051da36ac0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb95c88c-49bb-5efb-9ee9-31b82db8dea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334370" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32f71bb-d579-5a09-adf4-c4e8b8941ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156599} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a1db3b-fcf8-51cf-b063-025595438bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156600} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc869c3-e5e1-5d34-8018-4c693e813254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156601} {:answer/id #uuid "138b1df1-7ce1-518a-a848-5ec59fccc910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156602} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf5e3a4-2f1c-501a-ac3f-2d9d58d963a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "138b1df1-7ce1-518a-a848-5ec59fccc910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156602} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e1fb37-faf4-5c32-b77c-b90a3655ca33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8d33409-c623-5f70-8121-08ea4427ad09" :question/math-academy-id "q-88206" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504ba205-c349-541c-b744-4c22e4694fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291850} {:answer/id #uuid "cc917853-2cff-50ea-b159-bcf4beeaf0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291851} {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} {:answer/id #uuid "00023582-f48e-51ee-94c5-189c2664b183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291853} {:answer/id #uuid "9d256e7f-9c96-5c13-beb9-4be28f2c02c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e12088-410d-56ff-8b61-0d283d8401f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8db7c7d-2c36-54ae-a136-cfed51754274" :question/math-academy-id "q-88208" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4ad931-d6c5-5a3d-914c-c276a009d84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156611} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaf6f33-be77-5b1a-8c72-98ed6a1a8fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156612} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc0cd67-7df9-568a-bc9b-0c7d65892443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156613} {:answer/id #uuid "e35981b8-f69e-5e88-946e-767277ca3b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156614} {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615} :answer-field/id #uuid "799ba412-3bb6-5597-813b-29c7be335d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e1f8f9-d432-5676-a7be-df1e563ec326" :question/math-academy-id "q-88209" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fee84d-26d4-587a-9101-3467acb0bdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291857} {:answer/id #uuid "cce83b5c-5749-5141-ae7a-1f757dca84d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291858} {:answer/id #uuid "5135047c-12d3-5d92-a238-a70546662a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291859} {:answer/id #uuid "1246e48f-6ab3-5c56-814c-743fa9aee4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291860} {:answer/id #uuid "b787cb35-86ca-587e-b602-47de9cfaa5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce83b5c-5749-5141-ae7a-1f757dca84d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291858} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c08ecec-2526-5fc4-a6e7-8e8bd5986865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44ef4d16-5b7c-5c1c-ae5e-3f46913cf425" :question/math-academy-id "q-88582" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{2}-3$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =((-x)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({x}^{2}-3)$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f216eece-2660-5e20-9d7a-870c2f0e3afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291864} {:answer/id #uuid "3baddf13-f0b6-5ace-9873-99dc05236d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291865} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4e5687-aa81-58b3-aa6c-3b379ddfe892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291866} {:answer/id #uuid "b526c854-3d10-5f5e-9ef9-67e95870a066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291867} {:answer/id #uuid "26faacf8-1d22-5b0d-8a19-66e6dba74e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f216eece-2660-5e20-9d7a-870c2f0e3afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291864} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb72f35-3021-589c-bada-e1dd8e941a1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e5fda12-e16b-50c6-8bc6-86826ee8106a" :question/math-academy-id "q-88585" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡((-x)^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f05c1cc-9601-58e1-b22b-0c9137e0eba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291871} {:answer/id #uuid "efce043f-c81a-5694-8512-ac98dc0580d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291872} {:answer/id #uuid "71b9adf1-11c1-506e-a9c7-fc16dc13129e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291873} {:answer/id #uuid "3b36ea2c-51c5-55cb-9634-5f1fc7cf3ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291874} {:answer/id #uuid "89c5a5f4-5603-5cf5-ba87-8d510cdf568f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b9adf1-11c1-506e-a9c7-fc16dc13129e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291873} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8acd680-1834-52d3-a1e2-a9009635a9d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5b8f26e-0fd6-5a4b-9564-e6d55b96cd5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88689" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e05cf006-4e35-5836-9c93-3b8d7516c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291878} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2aa493-2f19-5559-b2b5-b6fc6d11ec3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291879} {:answer/id #uuid "867a39fb-d4e9-5177-b01d-2d8e24a09ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291880} {:answer/id #uuid "4da94543-36fc-50ab-9e04-f6a5248a7c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291881} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec32f9c-c87d-5acd-b4c3-87cd8d707d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec32f9c-c87d-5acd-b4c3-87cd8d707d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291882} :answer-field/id #uuid "f459767b-797d-5350-848a-cf0ecc8e9d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c4ec3cb-2a21-5f14-9f3d-91d3ab95f323" :question/math-academy-id "q-88698" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
